WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.400
Read the room first: function, balance and colour.

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:09.700
This is the video for module one of Interior Decoration Basics: how decorators see a room.

3
00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:19.400
Professionals start with three questions. First, what is this room for? Sleeping, receiving guests, studying, or selling to customers?

4
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.490
Second, who uses it, and when?

5
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.880
Third, what is not working now? Too dark, too crowded, too hot, nowhere to put things?

6
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.670
Every choice you make later must answer one of these questions.

7
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:45.520
Before you move a single chair, photograph the room from every corner and the doorway. Then check five things. Light: where do the windows face, and which bulbs are fitted?

8
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:51.290
Flow: can people walk from the door to the seats without squeezing past furniture?

9
00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:59.350
Clutter: what is on the floor and on top of cabinets? Clutter is often a storage problem, not a style problem.

10
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.690
Colour and condition: stains, peeling paint, faded curtains.

11
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:13.450
And climate: heat, dust, power cuts and cleaning. A white fabric sofa near a dusty road will not stay white.

12
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:23.260
Ngozi's neighbour asks her to make the parlour nicer. The parlour is used for visitors in the day and family TV in the evening.

13
00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:31.160
There is one window, and one ceiling bulb with a cool white light, so the corners are dark after 6 pm.

14
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:37.390
A large centre table blocks the route from the door, so people walk sideways past it.

15
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.240
School bags and shoes pile up by the door, because there is nowhere to put them.

16
00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:54.200
The walls have marks at hand height and the curtains are faded. So her plan starts with flow, storage, light and curtains, not with buying new decorations.

17
00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:11.470
Every object has visual weight. Large, dark or bright things look heavier. A room feels balanced when that weight is spread so no side seems to tip over. Symmetrical balance is a mirror image on both sides. It feels formal and calm.

18
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:28.740
Asymmetrical balance uses different items of equal weight, like one sofa balanced by two chairs. Halima's client had all the heavy furniture on one wall. Moving the tall cabinet to the opposite wall balanced the room without buying anything.

19
00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:36.140
A centre table is often about two-thirds the length of the sofa, so people can reach it from each seat.

20
00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:48.220
Give the room one focal point, like a feature wall, a framed adire panel, or a neatly styled TV wall. Arrange seats to face it, and keep other walls quieter.

21
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:54.170
Repeat each accent colour at least three times, so the eye moves around the room.

22
00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:01.600
Avoid rugs that float. Ideally at least the front legs of the sofa and chairs rest on the rug.

23
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:09.680
And do not hang pictures too high. Many decorators aim for the centre at about 1.5 m from the floor.

24
00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:25.010
Divide a room's colour roughly into 60% dominant colour, usually the walls. Bisi's client loved an indigo adire fabric with rust-orange details, so Bisi chose soft, warm off-white walls.

25
00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.590
30% secondary colour: indigo adire curtains and a mid-blue bedspread.

26
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:39.840
10% accent: rust-orange cushions, a clay pot and a picture frame, repeating the accent three times.

27
00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:50.160
Light changes colour, so paint a patch about the size of an A3 sheet and look at it in the morning, afternoon and at night before the client chooses.

28
00:03:51.410 --> 00:04:07.150
You are learning decoration, not structural work. Moving sockets, adding ceiling lights, removing walls or changing plumbing must be done by qualified people. You can coordinate them, but say in writing that their work is their responsibility.

29
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.250
Keep windows and doors open while painting and until the smell fades, and take breaks in fresh air.

30
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:21.530
Keep paint and thinners away from children and from flames, and read the safety instructions on the tin.

31
00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:39.130
To remember. Start with function, users and problems. Take before photos from every corner. Spread the visual weight and give the room one focal point. Use 60-30-10. And test real paint in morning, afternoon and night light.

32
00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:53.990
Now practise. Assess one real room with photos and the five-point check. Rebalance a room on paper using a black and white photo. And build a colour scheme from something the owner loves, with real samples.

33
00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.280
Continue with the lessons below.
